# MAGIC <div class="tbl"><table>
# MAGIC <thead>
# MAGIC <tr>
# MAGIC <th>Building block</th>
# MAGIC <th>What it is</th>
# MAGIC </tr>
# MAGIC </thead>
# MAGIC <tbody>
# MAGIC <tr>
# MAGIC <td><strong>Job</strong></td>
# MAGIC <td>A named workflow with triggers, parameters, notifications and permissions</td>
# MAGIC </tr>
# MAGIC <tr>
# MAGIC <td><strong>Task</strong></td>
# MAGIC <td>One unit of work: notebook, Python script/wheel, <strong>SQL</strong>, <strong>dashboard</strong>, <strong>pipeline</strong>, dbt, <strong>Run Job</strong>, <strong>If/else</strong>, <strong>For each</strong></td>
# MAGIC </tr>
# MAGIC <tr>
# MAGIC <td><strong>Dependency</strong></td>
# MAGIC <td>"Depends on" edges between tasks. Tasks without dependencies run <strong>in parallel</strong></td>
# MAGIC </tr>
# MAGIC <tr>
# MAGIC <td><strong>Compute</strong></td>
# MAGIC <td><strong>Serverless</strong> (recommended), a <strong>job cluster</strong> that tasks of the job can share, or a <strong>SQL warehouse</strong> for SQL and dashboard tasks</td>
# MAGIC </tr>
# MAGIC </tbody>
# MAGIC </table></div>
# MAGIC <p>Job clusters are created for the run and terminated afterwards. They are cheaper than all-purpose clusters, which are meant for interactive development.</p>
